A class is a blueprint for creating objects that bundle data (attributes) and behavior (methods) together:

 class Trail:
 def __init__(self, name, distance_km):
 self.name = name
 self.distance_km = distance_km

 def describe(self):
 return f"{self.name} is {self.distance_km} km long."

`__init__` runs automatically when you create a new object (`el_yunque = Trail("El Yunque Loop", 6.2)`), setting up its starting attributes. `self` refers to the specific object the method is being called on — it's always the first parameter of an instance method, though Python passes it in automatically when you call `el_yunque.describe()`.

Classes support inheritance: a more specific class can build on a general one and reuse its behavior.

 class CoastalTrail(Trail):
 def __init__(self, name, distance_km, tide_dependent):
 super().__init__(name, distance_km)
 self.tide_dependent = tide_dependent

`super().__init__(...)` calls the parent class's setup so you don't have to repeat it. Inheritance is most useful when several classes genuinely share behavior — reach for it when you notice real duplication, not just because two things sound similar.

— Based on: Python Crash Course ch.9

A test is just code that checks other code. Python's built-in `unittest` module gives you a standard shape for this:

 import unittest

 def add(a, b):
 return a + b

 class TestAdd(unittest.TestCase):
 def test_positive_numbers(self):
 self.assertEqual(add(2, 3), 5)

 if __name__ == "__main__":
 unittest.main()

`assertEqual`, `assertTrue`, `assertRaises`, and similar assertion methods each check a specific kind of expectation and report clearly when it fails, including exactly what was expected versus what actually happened.

Testing a class usually means checking its methods produce the right state changes — a `setUp()` method runs before every test in a class, useful for creating a fresh object each time so tests don't interfere with each other.

The habit worth building isn't "test everything exhaustively" — it's writing a test whenever you fix a bug (so it never silently comes back) and for any function whose correctness genuinely matters. Tests also double as living documentation: reading a well-named test tells you what a function is supposed to do faster than reading its implementation.

— Based on: Python Crash Course ch.11
